/-!
# Verified code generation from Lir to x86-64

A compiler from `Machine.Lir` programs over physical registers (for example the
output of `Lir.Alloc.spill`) to bytes of the handwritten local x86-64 model
`X86.Ext`, with a forward-simulation proof against the fetch/decode/execute
machine `Ext.step`. The model is written from the Intel SDM and is not derived
from, or proved to correspond with, the Sail, ACL2 or any hardware
description; every statement below is about that local model.

* `Regs`: register conventions. Nine allocatable registers
  (`rbx rbp rsi rdi r8 r9 r10 r12 r13`), scratch `rax rcx rdx r11` (which
  covers the fixed registers of shifts, DIV/IDIV and SETcc), `r15` the linear
  memory base, `r14` the stack limit.
* `Code`: selection, frames, System V argument/result registers (at most six
  arguments and two results, otherwise `Error`), call sites, prologue with a
  stack-limit check that traps with UD2, epilogue, the symbolic code and its
  layout with `rel32` displacements, and `compile`.
* `Layout`: where the code of each program point is.
* `Machine`, `LinMem`, `Seq`, `Stack`: the memory layout of a loaded program
  (`Space`, `Good`), straight-line execution (`runSeq`), stack words
  (`WordAt`), the linear memory relation (`MemRel`), and instruction facts.
* `Ops`: every supported operator is computed correctly by its sequence, for
  any aliasing of destination and sources; SETcc reads only flags defined by
  the preceding CMP; DIV/IDIV run only on operands for which Lir division is
  defined, where they cannot raise `#DE`.
* `Rel`: the state relation (`Env.Body`, `Env.Chain`, `Env.Entry`).
* `Step`, `Moves`, `Entry`, `Prologue`, `Call`, `Return`: simulation of each
  kind of Lir step.
* `Sim`: the `Lir.CaseSimulation`; `Correct`: the loaded machine and
  `compile_correct`; `Examples`: the compiled factorial and its correctness.

Every Lir operator is compiled. `mulhs` and `mulhu` use a sequence of 64-bit
IMULs on 32-bit halves (the model has no one-operand MUL/IMUL). `clz`, `ctz`,
`popcnt` and their 32-bit forms are compiled to LZCNT, TZCNT and POPCNT, whose
meaning in the model assumes the corresponding CPUID features (`usesBitCount`
reports whether a program uses them). `compile` reports an error for more than
six arguments or parameters, more than two results or returned values, missing
jump or call targets, a frame or program too large for 32-bit displacements.

What is proved is a forward simulation: returned, trapped and diverging Lir
runs are matched by the machine, up to a stop at a stack-limit check. Lir runs
that get stuck (undefined behaviour) carry no guarantee, and no statement is
made about machine runs from states that are not reached this way.
-/
